Now check Clause 1: $\neg x(0, 2, 0) \lor \neg x(1, 1, 1)$.
We have $x(0, 2, 0)=T, x(1, 1, 1)=T$.
So $\neg T \lor \neg T \implies F \lor F \implies F$. **Contradiction!**
Clause 1 requires $\neg x(0, 2, 0) \lor \neg x(1, 1, 1)$.
If $x(0, 2, 0)=T$, then $\neg x(0, 2, 0)$ is False.
So we must have $\neg x(1, 1, 1) = T$, i.e., $x(1, 1, 1) = F$.
So $x(1, 1, 1)$ must be False.
Now check Clause 2: $x(0, 2, 0) \lor x(1, 1, 1)$.
We have $T \lor F \implies T$. (Satisfied)
Check Clause 3: $x(2, 2, 0) \lor \neg x(1, 1, 1)$.
We have $x(2, 2, 0)$ is free? No, $x(2, 2, 0)$ is part of the clause.
Wait, Clause 3 is $x(2, 2, 0) \lor \neg x(1, 1, 1)$.
We found $x(1, 1, 1) = F$, so $\neg x(1, 1, 1) = T$.
Clause 3 is satisfied regardless of $x(2, 2, 0)$.
So far: $x(0, 2, 0)=T, x(1, 1, 1)=F$.
Check Clause 7: $x(0, 2, 0) \lor \neg x(2, 2, 0)$.
We have $T \lor \neg x(2, 2, 0)$. This is always True. (Satisfied)
Check Clause 5: $\neg x(0, 2, 0) \lor x(2, 2, 0)$.
We have $\neg T \lor x(2, 2, 0) \implies F \lor x(2, 2, 0) \implies x(2, 2, 0)$.
So $x(2, 2, 0)$ must be True.
Check Clause 6: $\neg x(2, 2, 0) \lor x(1, 1, 1)$.
We have $\neg T \lor F \implies F \lor F \implies F$. **Contradiction!**
Clause 6 requires $\neg x(2, 2, 0) \lor x(1, 1, 1)$.
We found $x(2, 2, 0)=T$ (so $\neg x(2, 2, 0)=F$).
We found $x(1, 1, 1)=F$ (so $x(1, 1, 1)=F$).
Thus $F \lor F = F$. **Contradiction.**

**Conclusion:**
The system is logically inconsistent. There is no assignment of truth values to the variables that satisfies all 7 conditions simultaneously. The Z3 certificate correctly identifies this as unsatisfiable.

